The same leverage works upward. When a supplier fills existing factories and spreads engineering and support costs across more revenue, margins can expand. But if it builds too much capacity near the top of the cycle, the added cost base can make the downturn worse.

The capacity cycle feeds on its own success

When one missing tool can delay a multibillion-dollar fab, customers have a strong reason to order early. Long lead times encourage suppliers to buy components, add workers and help critical vendors expand. Strong bookings therefore create more future supply.

The cycle turns when that supply arrives faster than customers need it. A chipmaker with enough installed capacity can pause new purchases even if long-term chip demand remains healthy. Equipment revenue may stay strong for a while as old backlog ships, then weaken after new orders have already slowed.

This is why the semiconductor equipment cycle can mislead both operators and investors. Current shipments describe decisions made earlier. Current orders describe expectations about future capacity. Neither alone proves that the end market can absorb all the output being built.

Who keeps the economics?

A supplier is more likely to preserve profits when its tool removes a hard production bottleneck, has few qualified alternatives and is costly to replace in an established process. Pricing, market share and service revenue then have more support.

KLA’s fiscal 2026 filing illustrates several pieces of this structure. TSMC accounted for more than 10% of KLA revenue, showing the bargaining and timing risk created by large customers. Services were about 23% of total revenue, providing work tied to the installed base rather than only new-system purchases. KLA also reported a 61.3% gross margin, but explained that margin still moves with volume, product mix, manufacturing efficiency, installation, warranty and support costs.

A strong product position therefore does not abolish the cycle. It can make the supplier more resilient inside the cycle.

Prediction check

A supplier’s backlog rises 50%, lead times extend and management expands production. At the same time, customer factory utilization is flat and chip inventories are rising. Is the supplier’s future profit now safer?

Answer: not necessarily. The backlog may contain orders placed to reserve delivery slots rather than meet near-term production needs. Rising inventories and flat utilization weaken the reason to install more capacity, while the supplier’s expansion raises inventory and fixed costs. The conclusion would improve if the orders served an unavoidable technology transition, customers’ sites were ready, cancellation protection was strong and the supplier had scarce, qualified capability.

Read the cycle in causal order

Start with final demand and useful AI output. Then check chip inventory and factory utilization, customer capital budgets, supplier orders, backlog quality, shipments and acceptance. Finally examine pricing, gross margin, service mix, working capital and the supplier’s own capacity additions.

The reusable principle is simple: demand growth creates an opportunity, not a guaranteed return. Durable supplier profit requires a real bottleneck, defensible positioning, high-quality orders and enough discipline to avoid building tomorrow’s excess capacity during today’s shortage.

